Yes, that's a common use case for geolocation services. Here's how you can use the Geolocation API to track someone's location:

1. Install the Geolocation API:

```javascript
const geolocation = require('@google-cloud/geolocation');
```

2. Create a new client:

```javascript
const client = new geolocation.Client({
  projectId: 'YOUR_PROJECT_ID',
  locationId: 'YOUR_LOCATION_ID',
});
```

3. Set up the geolocation request:

```javascript
const location = {
  lat: 40.7127,
  lng: -74.005,
};

client.getLocation(location).then((result) => {
  console.log(result.location);
});
```

4. Get the location:

```javascript
client.getLocation(location).then((result) => {
  console.log(result.location);
});
```

5. Display the location:

```javascript
console.log(result.location);
```

This will display the user's current location on a map. You can customize the map style and other options to suit your needs.
